import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.DycPnLPt.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"The facilitator","description":"What the facilitator does, why it is non-custodial, where your API key fits, the guarantees verify enforces, and what settlement does on Canton.","frontmatter":{"description":"What the facilitator does, why it is non-custodial, where your API key fits, the guarantees verify enforces, and what settlement does on Canton."},"headers":[],"relativePath":"concepts/facilitator.md","filePath":"concepts/facilitator.md"}'),r={name:"concepts/facilitator.md"};function i(s,e,d,c,h,l){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="the-facilitator" tabindex="-1">The facilitator <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-facilitator" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The facilitator&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>x402</code> makes an ordinary HTTP request payable: a client that calls a paid endpoint gets back <code>402 Payment Required</code>, signs a payment, and retries with it. The <strong>facilitator</strong> is the HTTP service that turns that signed payment into a real, settled payment on the Canton Network — so your server never has to speak to a ledger, hold a wallet, or run a node. Your server (the resource server) just hands each payment to the facilitator, which does exactly two things with it: <code>POST /v2/verify</code> checks a signed payment against your terms without submitting or reserving anything; <code>POST /v2/settle</code> then executes it on Canton and returns the update id.</p><h2 id="non-custodial-by-construction" tabindex="-1">Non-custodial by construction <a class="header-anchor" href="#non-custodial-by-construction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Non-custodial by construction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Canton settles in three steps — <code>prepare</code>, <code>sign</code>, <code>execute</code> — and that split is what makes the facilitator non-custodial. The payer does <code>prepare</code> and <code>sign</code> with its own key, without going through the facilitator. The facilitator only ever performs the final <code>execute</code> of a transaction the payer already signed: it never sees a private key, and it cannot move funds without that signature.</p><h2 id="the-payment-scheme" tabindex="-1">The payment scheme <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-payment-scheme" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The payment scheme&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><code>exact</code> on a <code>canton:*</code> network — the payer transfers Canton Coin (Amulet) directly to your party; you receive CC.</li></ul><p><a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-152/reference/http-api.html#get-v2-supported"><code>GET /v2/supported</code></a> is the authority on what a given facilitator accepts — the network id and the asset to pay in.</p><h2 id="how-you-get-paid" tabindex="-1">How you get paid <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-you-get-paid" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;How you get paid&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Settlements land directly at your <strong>Canton party</strong> — the payout party of the project your API key belongs to. The payer transfers Amulet (CC) straight to it; the facilitator holds no balance, so there is nothing to withdraw. That requires your payout party to hold a live <code>TransferPreapproval</code>. Without one, Amulet cannot pay it directly — the Token Standard turns the transfer into an offer the party must accept — so the facilitator <strong>refuses</strong> the payment rather than relaying one that lands somewhere the payer can still withdraw it (see <a href="#what-settlement-does">What settlement does</a>). Completed settlements show up on the Settlements page of the merchant dashboard.</p><h2 id="your-api-key-is-the-facilitator-credential" tabindex="-1">Your API key is the facilitator credential <a class="header-anchor" href="#your-api-key-is-the-facilitator-credential" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Your API key is the facilitator credential&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A key you create on the API keys page of the merchant dashboard is what your server sends as <code>Authorization: Bearer</code> to <code>/v2/verify</code> and <code>/v2/settle</code>. You can create and rotate keys in the dashboard yourself — no facilitator redeploy — and the key itself is never stored: what is kept is its hash, plus its <code>sk_live_</code>/<code>sk_test_</code> prefix and last four characters so you can tell your keys apart. A revocation can take up to a couple of minutes to take effect.</p><h2 id="what-verify-guarantees" tabindex="-1">What verify guarantees <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-verify-guarantees" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What verify guarantees&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Steps 5 and 6 of <a href="/canton-x402-sdk/pr-152/concepts/payment-flow.html">the payment flow</a> are the facilitator&#39;s half. Before any money moves, verify enforces a handful of guarantees — your payment can&#39;t be replayed, tampered with, or under-paid — and settle re-runs all of them before executing on Canton. The facilitator never moves a payer&#39;s funds on its own authority: the only write that moves them is <code>executeAndWaitForTransaction</code> of a <strong>payer-signed</strong> transaction.</p><p>These run in order, with no chain write. The first seven are shared code — the same <code>@chainsafe/x402-core</code> verifier the SDK exposes — so a payer can pre-flight its payload against them, and they refuse with the Canton scheme spec&#39;s codes, saying in <code>extensions.detail</code> which of a code&#39;s causes it was. The last is the facilitator&#39;s alone, since only it holds the state involved:</p><ol><li><strong>Right scheme and network</strong> — the payload, the requirements, and the network this facilitator is bound to all agree (<code>unsupported_scheme</code> or <code>invalid_network</code> otherwise).</li><li><strong>The transaction decodes</strong> — <code>preparedTransaction</code> is one gzip member within the size caps, its node graph is well-formed, and it holds the one transfer. Its sender is the payer (the payload names none), and checks 3 to 6 report it. A payload missing its proof is <code>invalid_exact_canton_missing_proof</code>, and one that does not decode is <code>invalid_exact_canton_malformed_payload</code>.</li><li><strong>Still executable</strong> — the signed transfer&#39;s own <code>executeBefore</code> is far enough in the future to settle (<code>invalid_exact_canton_expired</code>). The quote itself no longer expires; the deadline that counts is inside what the payer signed.</li><li><strong>The keys belong to the payer</strong> — the payload carries none. The facilitator reads the sender party&#39;s signing keys from the ledger, by that party id, and it is the lookup that binds them to the payer, so a caller can&#39;t sign as a party it doesn&#39;t control (<code>invalid_exact_canton_signature_invalid</code> when none can be resolved).</li><li><strong>The signature is valid</strong> — the hash recomputed from the transaction itself must equal <code>preparedTxHash</code>, and the payer&#39;s key must have signed it (<code>invalid_exact_canton_signature_invalid</code> otherwise).</li><li><strong>The signed transfer actually pays you.</strong> This is what binds the payment to your terms: the transfer&#39;s receiver, instrument and amount are checked against the requirements, so a valid signature over a divergent transfer is rejected: <code>invalid_exact_canton_merchant_mismatch</code>, <code>invalid_exact_canton_instrument_id_mismatch</code>, <code>invalid_exact_canton_amount_mismatch</code> or <code>invalid_exact_canton_memo_mismatch</code>. The amount must be EQUAL, not merely sufficient — <code>exact</code> is the scheme&#39;s distinguishing property, and a payer who overpays has no refund path. The transfer&#39;s <code>x402.memo</code> must equal your <code>extra.memo</code> exactly — in both directions, so a transfer carrying a memo you did not quote is refused as well.</li><li><strong>The transfer went through a real factory</strong> — the contract the transfer choice was exercised on must be one the transaction itself discloses, exercised through the Token Standard <code>TransferFactory</code> interface, and carrying the instrument&#39;s admin among its signatories (<code>invalid_exact_canton_malformed_payload</code>, with a <code>factory_</code> detail naming which part failed). Without it a payer could exercise a contract of their own that merely exposes a choice of the same name, and the payment would reduce to &quot;something called transfer happened&quot;. The disclosure travels inside what the payer signed, so this check takes it on the payer&#39;s word; the synchronizer authenticates it against the contract id at submission, which is where a fabricated one fails.</li><li><strong>No replay, and you&#39;re an allowed destination</strong> — the payment has not settled before (the ledger is the real guard: a replayed transfer names input holdings that are already spent), and for an authenticated merchant <code>payTo</code> must be a party that key is allowed to receive at (your destination policy). A destination denial comes back as <code>invalid_exact_canton_merchant_not_registered</code> with a <code>destination_not_allowed</code> detail, on verify and on settle alike.</li></ol><h2 id="what-settlement-does" tabindex="-1">What settlement does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-settlement-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What settlement does&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Funds are real Splice <strong>Amulet</strong> (Canton Coin), transferred through the Token Standard&#39;s <code>TransferFactory</code> in one atomic Canton transaction. Your party must hold a live <code>TransferPreapproval</code>: with one the transfer is direct, so nothing is escrowed or locked up. Without one the Token Standard would make it an offer that completes only once you accept it — which is not a payment the facilitator can stand behind, since the resource is already served while the funds sit where the payer can withdraw them. Such a payment is refused at verify, with <code>invalid_exact_canton_preapproval_missing</code>. What is recorded is the signed transfer&#39;s amount, not the quoted floor, so an overpayment is not understated.</p>',17)])])}const y=t(r,[["render",i]]);export{p as __pageData,y as default};
